{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md\nsed -n '1,280p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md\nwc -l collab/1788876999259-architect-v2-complete-retention-transport.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\nnl -ba collab/1788876999259-architect-v2-complete-retention-transport.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '1,260p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md\nsed -n '1,280p' .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md\nwc -l collab/1788876999259-architect-v2-complete-retention-transport.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\nnl -ba collab/1788876999259-architect-v2-complete-retention-transport.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md | sed -n '1,260p'\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n     210 collab/1788876999259-architect-v2-complete-retention-transport.claude-fable-5-1.findings.md\n     1\t---\n     2\t\n     3\tCompleted-GMT: 2026-09-08 14:35 GMT\n     4\tCompleted-Local: 2026-09-08 21:35 +0700 (Asia/Bangkok)\n     5\tCoding-Agent: claude (Opus 5)\n     6\tSession-ID: 0dae45ea-4204-49d9-bf35-c56e24cce14e\n     7\t\n     8\t# A complete-retention DaoStream v2 transport\n     9\t\n    10\t## 0. The scoping judgement: confirmed\n    11\t\n    12\tVerified against the tree, not taken on report.\n    13\t\n    14\t- v1 local streams are opened bare: `(ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :ringbuffer})` with no `:capacity` — `test/dao/space/transactor_test.cljc:219` and fifteen further sites, `test/dao/space/query_test.cljc:569`, `test/dao/space/stigmergy_test.clj:103`. `dao.space` itself never creates one; the transactor takes it as `:local-stream`, \"supplied, never created, registered, or closed\" (`src/cljc/dao/space/transactor.cljc:8-14`).\n    15\t- v1 eviction is guarded by `(and capacity …)` (`src/cljc/dao/stream/ringbuffer.cljc:76-91`), so a nil capacity short-circuits the branch: `:head` never advances, nothing is evicted, `:full` is unreachable and `:daostream/gap` is unreachable.\n    16\t- Nothing persists that stream. `publish-index!` (`src/cljc/dao/space/index.cljc:540-604`) snapshots it from position 0 (`snapshot-datoms`, `:442-468`) and emits b-tree blobs to a **v2** DaoJing intake stream; durability is entirely on the jing side. The code already says why complete retention is load-bearing: \"Because the build starts at cursor position zero and reconstructs complete indexes, local-stream must retain its complete datom history\" (`index.cljc:561-564`). `derive-next-t` (`transactor.cljc:125-139`) and `schema/proposed-schema` (`schema.cljc:926`) rescan from zero for the same reason.\n    17\t\n    18\tSo the local stream is in-memory, unbounded, append-only, and dies with the process; a restarted process today derives `next-t` = 0 from an empty stream. **The faithful v2 counterpart is an in-memory, unbounded, append-only log.** A file-backed durable log would add semantics `dao.space` does not have today and has not specified — it would silently change what a restart means — and durability across restarts is `dao.jing`'s job, which `dao.space` already composes with for publication. The judgement stands.\n    19\t\n    20\tWhat `dao.space` actually requires, exhaustively: reader and writer surfaces; a cursor at the origin that stays at the origin; `next` returning values in order and never `gap`; `append!` returning `ok`. Nothing else. It needs no attachments (one process, handle passed directly), and its `ds/closed?` guards (`schema.cljc:318-326`, `:1069`, `:1127`) are the transactor plan's problem — v2 has no `closed?` predicate and this transport must not grow one.\n    21\t\n    22\t## 1. Name and namespace\n    23\t\n    24\t- Namespace: **`dao.stream.memory-log`** → `src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc`\n    25\t- Transport type: **`:dao.stream/memory-log`**\n    26\t\n    27\tThe name states both halves of its nature, medium and retention, so it is mistaken for neither the ring buffer nor a durable log. v1's `dao.stream.file` was the durable one and this is not that; a later durable transport takes `dao.stream.file-log` / `:dao.stream/file-log`. Bare `:dao.stream/log` was rejected: it says complete retention and says nothing about surviving a restart, which is exactly the confusion §6 exists to prevent.\n    28\t\n    29\tThe creation specification is `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/memory-log}` with **no transport-owned keys**. There is deliberately no capacity key: capacity is the knob the contract just disowned for this purpose (`dao.stream.md:707-709`), and offering one invites the sizing mistake back in.\n    30\t\n    31\t## 2. The manifest\n    32\t\n    33\tDeclared operations are `create!`, `descriptor`, `cursor`, `next`, `append!`, `close!`. **`attach!` is absent, not excluded** — the transport has no attach implementation, so there is no operation whose outcomes to declare. `validate-manifest` (`conformance.cljc:24`) requires only `:descriptor` plus the operations its declared surfaces license, so an absent `:attach!` key is valid. A host dispatch table supplies no `:dao.stream/attach` for this type and `host-dispatch-attach!` answers `:dao.stream/not-found` — the contract's own answer for a descriptor with nothing behind it (`dao.stream.md:233-239`).\n    34\t\n    35\t```clojure\n    36\t(def memory-log-manifest\n    37\t  {:dao.stream/type log/transport-type\n    38\t   :surfaces #{:reader :writer :closable}\n    39\t   :retention :complete\n    40\t   :handle-factory handle\n    41\t   :operations\n    42\t   {:create!\n    43\t    {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/invalid-spec}\n    44\t     :exclusions\n    45\t     {:dao.stream/not-found\n    46\t      \"host dispatch selects the transport; a call reaching this handler has already matched it\"\n    47\t      :dao.stream/transport-error\n    48\t      \"creation allocates one in-memory state value and has no operational failure channel\"}}\n    49\t\n    50\t    :descriptor {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok} :exclusions {}}\n    51\t\n    52\t    :cursor\n    53\t    {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/invalid-anchor}\n    54\t     :exclusions\n    55\t     {:dao.stream/closed\n    56\t      \"there are no attachments: every handle is the logical stream's owner, and an owner mints cursors after close so retained history stays readable\"\n    57\t      :dao.stream/transport-error\n    58\t      \"minting reads one in-memory state value and cannot fail operationally\"}}\n    59\t\n    60\t    :next\n    61\t    {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/blocked :dao.stream/end\n    62\t                 :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch :dao.stream/invalid-cursor}\n    63\t     :exclusions\n    64\t     {:dao.stream/gap\n    65\t      \"retention is complete: no position is ever dropped, so no cursor can point at one that was\"\n    66\t      :dao.stream/transport-error\n    67\t      \"coherent in-memory state has no failure channel\"}}\n    68\t\n    69\t    :append!\n    70\t    {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/closed}\n    71\t     :exclusions\n    72\t     {:dao.stream/full\n    73\t      \"capacity is genuinely unbounded: the transport imposes no bound of its own, and the host heap is not a bound it can observe or refuse at\"\n    74\t      :dao.stream/invalid-value\n    75\t      \"the log holds host values by reference and encodes nothing, so no value is uncarriable\"\n    76\t      :dao.stream/transport-error\n    77\t      \"coherent in-memory state has no failure channel\"}}\n    78\t\n    79\t    :close! {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok} :exclusions {}}}\n    80\t\n    81\t   :fixtures\n    82\t   {:create! {:dao.stream/ok #(log/create! spec)\n    83\t              :dao.stream/invalid-spec #(log/create! {:dao.stream/type :other})}\n    84\t    :descriptor {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/descriptor (handle))}\n    85\t    :cursor {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/cursor (handle) :dao.stream/oldest)\n    86\t             :dao.stream/invalid-anchor #(stream/cursor (handle) ::invalid-anchor)}\n    87\t    :next {:dao.stream/ok #(let [h (handle) c (cur h :dao.stream/oldest)]\n    88\t                             (stream/append! h :value)\n    89\t                             (stream/next h c))\n    90\t           :dao.stream/blocked #(let [h (handle)]\n    91\t                                  (stream/next h (cur h :dao.stream/newest)))\n    92\t           :dao.stream/end #(let [h (handle) c (cur h :dao.stream/newest)]\n    93\t                              (stream/close! h)\n    94\t                              (stream/next h c))\n    95\t           :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch #(let [l (handle) r (handle)]\n    96\t                                          (stream/next r (cur l :dao.stream/oldest)))\n    97\t           :dao.stream/invalid-cursor #(stream/next (handle) nil)}\n    98\t    :append! {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/append! (handle) :value)\n    99\t              :dao.stream/closed #(let [h (handle)]\n   100\t                                    (stream/close! h)\n   101\t                                    (stream/append! h :value))}\n   102\t    :close! {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/close! (handle))}}})\n   103\t```\n   104\t\n   105\t`gap`'s exclusion reason is the contract's own first honest reason in substance: the outcome's precondition is impossible by the transport's nature (`dao.stream.md:442-445`). `:retention :complete` is a new manifest key; §4 says what it buys.\n   106\t\n   107\t## 3. The `full` obligation\n   108\t\n   109\t**Decision: genuinely unbounded, and `full` is excluded too.**\n   110\t\n   111\t\"Genuinely unbounded\" can honestly mean exactly one thing here: *the transport imposes no bound of its own.* It never drops a value and never refuses one on account of a policy it chose, because it has chosen none — no capacity in the specification, no watermark in the state, no arithmetic anywhere in the namespace that could produce a refusal. That is a structural property of the code, not a promise about the machine.\n   112\t\n   113\tThe real bound is the host heap, and the heap is not the transport's to report:\n   114\t\n   115\t- It is shared by everything in the process. An append that exhausts it was not refused by the log; the process ran out of memory while allocating, exactly as it would have in any other data structure.\n   116\t- `:dao.stream/ok` followed by an OOM is indeed not an outcome — and it is not what happens. Heap exhaustion occurs *during* the append, so that call returns no result at all. There is no state in which this transport answers `ok` and then fails to have appended.\n   117\t- `transport-error` is not the honest dodge either. Catching `OutOfMemoryError` on the JVM to return data would assert a recoverability that does not exist, and JS and Dart offer no portable signal to catch. The exclusion is the same across all three hosts, which is what an exclusion must be.\n   118\t\n   119\tThe bounded alternative was considered and rejected on the merits. A configured maximum would make `full` **permanent**, not transient: a log never frees space, so the writer's only contract-sanctioned recourse — retry later — never comes. A setting whose only correct value is \"big enough\" is the sizing fallacy in a different costume, and it converts a heap risk into a transaction failure `dao.space` has no policy for.\n   120\t\n   121\t**What the transport does when the heap bound is reached: nothing.** It contains no code for that condition, and adding some would be fiction. Where a composition needs a policy it has one place to put it, above the log: publish a checkpoint and start a fresh stream. For `dao.space` that checkpoint already exists — `publish-index!` — but wiring rotation is the transactor plan's business, out of scope here and named so it is not mistaken for an oversight.\n   122\t\n   123\tNo transport-owned size or depth operation is added. The contract permits one (`dao.stream.md:400-405`) and it would be the first thing contract-generic code reached for.\n   124\t\n   125\t## 4. Conformance\n   126\t\n   127\t### What the suite already covers, and what changes\n   128\t\n   129\tThe manifest passes `validate-manifest` as written: `produces ∪ exclusions` equals `v2/operation-outcomes` per declared op, the sets are disjoint, every exclusion carries a non-empty reason.\n   130\t\n   131\tThree law blocks behave differently from the ring buffer's run:\n   132\t\n   133\t- `run-reader-laws` (`conformance.cljc:171`) mints `:oldest` on a fresh handle and reads it twice. Here `:oldest` is position 0 and stays there forever, so the block passes for a stronger reason than on the ring buffer, where the anchor tracks an advancing watermark.\n   134\t- `run-induction-coverage` (`:112`) needs no `gap` inducer, since `gap` is excluded — the ring buffer's `gap` fixture (`ringbuffer_test.cljc:134-138`) has no counterpart and must not be reproduced by contrivance.\n   135\t- `run-close-laws` (`:231`) closes the logical stream, not an attachment.\n   136\t\n   137\t**The abstract model needs no change.** `make-abstract-stream-model` (`:382`) already takes `capacity nil`, and its trim is guarded by `(and (:capacity state) …)` (`:481-485`). With no attachments, `visible-tail` is `:next-pos` and `retained-first` is 0 once anything is appended (`:next-pos`, also 0, when empty), so the `:oldest` expectation `(min (retained-first state) (visible-tail state inv))` (`:509`) is 0 at every state. That is the contract's \"a fresh `:oldest` *is* the origin\" already encoded in the model. Supply a `:cursor-projector` for this transport's cursor keys and `capacity nil`; nothing else.\n   138\t\n   139\tLinearizability histories to run, mirroring `bounded-concurrent-linearizability-histories` (`ringbuffer_test.cljc:299`): concurrent append/append, append/next, append/close, and two cursors at position 0 read concurrently after one completed append (reader independence, here through two cursors on the owner handle rather than two attachments). `run-concurrently` is private to `ringbuffer_test`; duplicate the twelve-line helper rather than promote it — consolidating the copies is a separate cleanup, not this transport's.\n   140\t\n   141\t### Whether the suite needs an addition: yes\n   142\t\n   143\tThe observation in the brief is exact, and worth stating as the asymmetry it is: `run-induction-coverage` verifies that every **declared** outcome is inducible, and its \"no observed outcome falls outside `:produces`\" check only inspects results from fixtures the manifest itself supplies. **No fixture can be written for an outcome the manifest excludes**, so an exclusion is today an unchecked assertion. Nothing in the harness can catch a transport that declares complete retention and evicts.\n   144\t\n   145\t\"Never evicts\" is a negative over an unbounded number of appends and is not testable in general. Its *observable consequence* is, and it is the one property that distinguishes this transport from every evicting one:\n   146\t\n   147\t> A `:dao.stream/oldest` cursor minted at any time is at the origin, and an origin cursor kept from before the first append replays every value in order without a `gap`.\n   148\t\n   149\tAdd both, in two places:\n   150\t\n   151\t1. **In `memory_log_test.cljc`** — a `complete-retention` deftest: mint an origin cursor, append N = 1000 values (past any plausible window), replay the kept cursor asserting every read is `ok`, values arrive in order, and no outcome is `gap`; then assert a freshly minted `:oldest` equals the origin cursor. This is `ringbuffer_test.cljc:23`'s `retention-and-gaps` inverted, and a ring buffer fails it.\n   152\t2. **In `conformance.cljc`** — a `run-retention-laws` block, ~25 lines, run from `run-conformance-suite` only when the manifest declares `:retention :complete`. It checks (a) that `:next` excludes `:dao.stream/gap` — the two declarations may not disagree — and (b) the origin property above over `:handle-factory`. This makes the declaration mean something for the next complete-retention transport as well as this one, and it is falsifiable in this repo's existing style (`linearizability-oracle-rejects-an-invalid-history`, `:261`): bolt `:retention :complete` onto the ring buffer manifest in a local `let` and the block must fail.\n   153\t\n   154\t`:retention :complete` is a **manifest** declaration — configuration provenance, which the contract permits — not a predicate on a handle, which it forbids (`dao.stream.md:764-767`). If a reviewer judges (2) harness creep, (1) alone suffices and (2) drops without changing the transport.\n   155\t\n   156\t## 5. Cursors\n   157\t\n   158\tState is the minimum that can express this transport:\n   159\t\n   160\t```clojure\n   161\t{:identity (str (random-uuid))   ; portable data, as in ringbuffer.cljc:27\n   162\t :values   []                    ; dense from position 0\n   163\t :closed?  false}\n   164\t```\n   165\t\n   166\tA **vector**, not the ring buffer's map: positions are dense and nothing is ever removed, so `conj` and `nth` suffice and the tail is `(count values)`. There is no `:first` watermark because there is nothing for one to watermark, and no `:attachments` because there are none. The absence of eviction is therefore structural — the namespace contains no expression that removes an element — rather than a policy the code chooses each append.\n   167\t\n   168\t- **Positions** are integers from 0, dense, absolute, never reused, never dropped.\n   169\t- **Cursor value**: `{:dao.stream.memory-log/identity <id> :dao.stream.memory-log/position <n>}`. The identity is the logical-stream identity — the same value `descriptor` projects under `:dao.stream/identity` and the same one `cursor-mismatch` compares — carried under a transport-owned key because cursor representation belongs to the transport.\n   170\t- **`:oldest`** is position 0, always, for the life of the stream, before the first append and after close. On this transport a fresh `:oldest` *is* the origin — exactly the contract's statement and exactly what `dao.space`'s from-zero rescans need.\n   171\t- **`:newest`** is `(count values)`.\n   172\t- **Attach and frozen tails: none.** There are no attachments, so `cursor` never answers `:dao.stream/closed` and no handle has a frozen visible tail. Every handle is the logical stream's owner.\n   173\t- **After close**, `cursor` keeps minting; `:oldest` is still 0, `:newest` still the tail, and a `next` past the tail answers `end` instead of `blocked`.\n   174\t- **`next`** validates in the ring buffer's order — not a map → `invalid-cursor`; missing identity key → `invalid-cursor`; identity mismatch → `cursor-mismatch`; non-integer position → `invalid-cursor`; then `pos < tail` → `ok` with value and successor; else `end` if closed, `blocked` otherwise. The `pos < first` branch that produces `gap` has no counterpart and is simply absent.\n   175\t- **`append!`** takes the ring buffer's single-linearization-point shape (`ringbuffer.cljc:117-132`): one `swap!` whose function checks `:closed?` and either records `closed` or `conj`s, with the outcome carried out in a `volatile!`. A deref before the swap would let an append that began before a close land after it.\n   176\t\n   177\t### Host portability\n   178\t\n   179\tThe transport source needs **no reader conditionals at all** — `deftype`, protocol implementation, `atom`/`swap!`, `volatile!`, `random-uuid`, `conj`, `nth`, `count` are all used unguarded by `ringbuffer.cljc` across clj, cljs and cljd today. That is the safest position with respect to the `#?(:clj …)` trap, since the trap only bites where a conditional is added. The one `#?` in this work is the JVM-thread block in the test, written `#?(:cljd nil :clj …)` with `:cljd` **first**, as `ringbuffer_test.cljc:252,277,297` already does.\n   180\t\n   181\t## 6. What it does not do\n   182\t\n   183\t- **No durability across process restart.** The log is one atom; the process dying takes it. A restarted host sees a new, empty logical stream with a new identity.\n   184\t- **No eviction, no `gap`, no `full`, and no capacity key.**\n   185\t- **No attachments**: no `attach!`, no attachment identities, no frozen tails, no `:dao.stream/closed` from `cursor`.\n   186\t- **No encoding.** Values are held by reference, so this is not a serialization boundary; a live handle can pass through it, and no value is uncarriable.\n   187\t- **No size, depth, or retention query**, and no `closed?` predicate.\n   188\t- **No readiness or waiter mechanism** — the contract has none.\n   189\t\n   190\tA later **durable** transport is a different transport, not this one grown. It would add: a medium that survives restart, and therefore an identity recovered from the medium rather than minted at creation; `attach!` resolving against the filesystem rather than a host directory; `:dao.stream/transport-error` on `cursor`, `next` and `append!`, since locating the earliest retained position on a durable log is itself a read that can fail — the contract names precisely this case (`dao.stream.md:483-488`); `:dao.stream/invalid-value`, because it encodes and is therefore a serialization boundary; a real `:dao.stream/full`, because a disk is genuinely bounded and its bound *is* the transport's to observe; and a `close!` that flushes, hence a named channel where flush failure surfaces (`dao.stream.md:583-586`). Nothing in `dao.stream.memory-log` should be shaped to anticipate any of that.\n   191\t\n   192\t## 7. Phasing and the handoff\n   193\t\n   194\t**Phase A (this plan, one commit).** Add `src/cljc/dao/stream/memory_log.cljc` and `test/dao/stream/memory_log_test.cljc`; add `run-retention-laws` to `test/dao/stream/conformance.cljc`. `dao.stream.ringbuffer` and `docs/design/dao.stream.md` are untouched. No new design doc: the namespace docstring and the manifest are this transport's specification, and `dao.stream.file.md` documents a far larger thing.\n   195\t\n   196\t**Phase B.** The transactor plan is revised against it.\n   197\t\n   198\tThe sentence the next brief can quote:\n   199\t\n   200\t> `dao.space`'s local stream is created from `{:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/memory-log}` by `dao.stream.memory-log/create!`, whose declared complete retention is what makes `derive-next-t` and `publish-index!`'s from-origin rebuild correct; wiring any evicting transport there is a host assembly defect.\n   201\t\n   202\tAnd what that plan must **not** ask of it: durability across restart, a size or retention query, a `closed?` predicate, or a capacity. If the transactor needs to bound growth, it rotates above the log after publishing; the transport has no opinion.\n   203\t\n   204\t## Verification\n   205\t\n   206\t- `bb test:clj` — JVM: conformance suite, retention laws, linearizability histories including the concurrent ones.\n   207\t- `bb test:cljs` — Node; confirm `Testing dao.stream.memory-log-test` appears in the output, since the shadow `:node-test` build discovers namespaces rather than reading an allowlist.\n   208\t- `bb test:cljd` — Dart; the same suite minus the thread-based block.\n   209\t- Falsification: temporarily bolt `:retention :complete` onto `ringbuffer-manifest` in a local `let` and confirm `run-retention-laws` fails. Do not commit that.\n   210\t- Regression: `dao.stream.ringbuffer-test` must pass unchanged — the conformance addition is licensed by a manifest key the ring buffer does not declare.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba test/dao/stream/conformance.cljc | sed -n '1,190p;190,370p;370,570p'\nnl -ba test/dao/stream/ringbuffer_test.cljc | sed -n '1,180p;230,340p'\nnl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '96,120p;225,245p;383,451p;471,535p;677,735p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba test/dao/stream/conformance.cljc | sed -n '1,190p;190,370p;370,570p'\nnl -ba test/dao/stream/ringbuffer_test.cljc | sed -n '1,180p;230,340p'\nnl -ba docs/design/dao.stream.md | sed -n '96,120p;225,245p;383,451p;471,535p;677,735p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t(ns dao.stream.conformance\n     2\t  \"Declaration-driven contract conformance harness and bounded concurrency oracle\n     3\t   for DaoStream v2 transports.\n     4\t\n     5\t   The harness runs licensed law blocks based on the transport manifest:\n     6\t     - Result shapes, generic envelope validation, and descriptor identity (universal)\n     7\t     - Reader laws (cursor provenance, non-destructive reading, anchors)\n     8\t     - Writer laws (append acceptance, sequence ordering)\n     9\t     - Close laws (idempotent close, frozen availability, post-close outcomes)\n    10\t     - Manifest outcome exhaustiveness and induction coverage\n    11\t\n    12\t   Concurrency oracle:\n    13\t     Bounded offline linearizability checking over recorded invocation intervals\n    14\t     respecting happens-before real-time precedence and checking against an\n    15\t     abstract sequential stream model.\"\n    16\t  (:require [clojure.set :as set]\n    17\t            [dao.stream :as v2]))\n    18\t\n    19\t\n    20\t;; =============================================================================\n    21\t;; Manifest Validation\n    22\t;; =============================================================================\n    23\t\n    24\t(defn validate-manifest\n    25\t  \"Validates that manifest adheres to the declaration-driven conformance contract:\n    26\t   - :dao.stream/type is a qualified keyword.\n    27\t   - :surfaces is a subset of #{:reader :writer :closable}.\n    28\t   - :descriptor is always declared in :operations.\n    29\t   - For every declared operation:\n    30\t       * :produces is a subset of the contract's outcome set for that op.\n    31\t       * :exclusions is a map of {excluded-outcome reason-string}.\n    32\t       * (union produces (keys exclusions)) == contract outcome set for op.\n    33\t       * (intersection produces (keys exclusions)) is empty.\n    34\t       * Every exclusion has a non-empty explanation reason.\n    35\t   - Declared surfaces require their corresponding operations:\n    36\t       * :reader requires :cursor and :next\n    37\t       * :writer requires :append!\n    38\t       * :closable requires :close!\n    39\t   Returns {:valid? true} or {:valid? false :errors [...]}.\"\n    40\t  [manifest]\n    41\t  (let [errors (atom [])\n    42\t        ttype (:dao.stream/type manifest)\n    43\t        surfaces (:surfaces manifest)\n    44\t        ops-decl (:operations manifest)]\n    45\t\n    46\t    (when-not (v2/qualified-keyword?* ttype)\n    47\t      (swap! errors conj {:error :invalid-transport-type :type ttype}))\n    48\t\n    49\t    (when-not (and (set? surfaces) (set/subset? surfaces v2/surfaces))\n    50\t      (swap! errors conj {:error :invalid-surfaces :surfaces surfaces :allowed v2/surfaces}))\n    51\t\n    52\t    (when-not (map? ops-decl)\n    53\t      (swap! errors conj {:error :missing-operations-map :operations ops-decl}))\n    54\t\n    55\t    ;; Universal operation: descriptor must always be declared\n    56\t    (when-not (contains? ops-decl :descriptor)\n    57\t      (swap! errors conj {:error :missing-descriptor-declaration}))\n    58\t\n    59\t    ;; Surface-licensed operations\n    60\t    (when (contains? surfaces :reader)\n    61\t      (when-not (contains? ops-decl :cursor)\n    62\t        (swap! errors conj {:error :missing-reader-operation :operation :cursor}))\n    63\t      (when-not (contains? ops-decl :next)\n    64\t        (swap! errors conj {:error :missing-reader-operation :operation :next})))\n    65\t\n    66\t    (when (contains? surfaces :writer)\n    67\t      (when-not (contains? ops-decl :append!)\n    68\t        (swap! errors conj {:error :missing-writer-operation :operation :append!})))\n    69\t\n    70\t    (when (contains? surfaces :closable)\n    71\t      (when-not (contains? ops-decl :close!)\n    72\t        (swap! errors conj {:error :missing-closable-operation :operation :close!})))\n    73\t\n    74\t    ;; Validate each operation's produces/exclusions partition\n    75\t    (doseq [[op-key decl] ops-decl]\n    76\t      (let [canon-op (v2/canonical-op op-key)\n    77\t            contract-outcomes (get v2/operation-outcomes canon-op)]\n    78\t        (if-not contract-outcomes\n    79\t          (swap! errors conj {:error :unknown-operation :operation op-key})\n    80\t          (let [produces (get decl :produces #{})\n    81\t                exclusions (get decl :exclusions {})\n    82\t                excluded-outcomes (set (keys exclusions))\n    83\t                overlap (set/intersection produces excluded-outcomes)\n    84\t                union-outcomes (set/union produces excluded-outcomes)]\n    85\t            (when-not (empty? overlap)\n    86\t              (swap! errors conj {:error :produces-exclusions-overlap\n    87\t                                  :operation canon-op\n    88\t                                  :overlap overlap}))\n    89\t            (when-not (= union-outcomes contract-outcomes)\n    90\t              (swap! errors conj {:error :incomplete-outcome-partition\n    91\t                                  :operation canon-op\n    92\t                                  :declared union-outcomes\n    93\t                                  :expected contract-outcomes\n    94\t                                  :missing (set/difference contract-outcomes union-outcomes)\n    95\t                                  :unexpected (set/difference union-outcomes contract-outcomes)}))\n    96\t            (doseq [[ex-outcome reason] exclusions]\n    97\t              (when-not (and (string? reason) (pos? (count reason)))\n    98\t                (swap! errors conj {:error :missing-exclusion-reason\n    99\t                                    :operation canon-op\n   100\t                                    :outcome ex-outcome\n   101\t                                    :reason reason})))))))\n   102\t\n   103\t    (if (empty? @errors)\n   104\t      {:valid? true}\n   105\t      {:valid? false :errors @errors})))\n   106\t\n   107\t\n   108\t;; =============================================================================\n   109\t;; Conformance Test Runner & Law Blocks\n   110\t;; =============================================================================\n   111\t\n   112\t(defn- run-induction-coverage\n   113\t  \"Asserts manifest induction:\n   114\t   1. For each declared outcome in :produces, the fixture induces it.\n   115\t   2. No observed outcome falls outside the declared :produces set.\n   116\t   3. Every produced result satisfies v2/valid-outcome?.\"\n   117\t  [manifest]\n   118\t  (let [violations (atom [])\n   119\t        fixtures (:fixtures manifest {})\n   120\t        ops-decl (:operations manifest {})]\n   121\t    (doseq [[op-key decl] ops-decl]\n   122\t      (let [canon-op (v2/canonical-op op-key)\n   123\t            produces (get decl :produces #{})\n   124\t            op-fixtures (get fixtures op-key {})]\n   125\t        (doseq [outcome produces]\n   126\t          (if-let [inducer (get op-fixtures outcome)]\n   127\t            (let [res (inducer)\n   128\t                  observed-outcome (:dao.stream/outcome res)]\n   129\t              ;; Check 1: Inducer actually produces the declared outcome\n   130\t              (when-not (= observed-outcome outcome)\n   131\t                (swap! violations conj {:check :induction-mismatch\n   132\t                                        :operation canon-op\n   133\t                                        :expected-outcome outcome\n   134\t                                        :observed-outcome observed-outcome\n   135\t                                        :result res}))\n   136\t              ;; Check 2: Result is not outside declared produces\n   137\t              (when-not (contains? produces observed-outcome)\n   138\t                (swap! violations conj {:check :undeclared-outcome-observed\n   139\t                                        :operation canon-op\n   140\t                                        :outcome observed-outcome\n   141\t                                        :allowed produces}))\n   142\t              ;; Check 3: Result map is valid (required keys present, open map)\n   143\t              (when-let [defect (v2/validate-outcome canon-op res)]\n   144\t                (swap! violations conj {:check :invalid-outcome-shape\n   145\t                                        :operation canon-op\n   146\t                                        :defect defect})))\n   147\t            (swap! violations conj {:check :missing-fixture-inducer\n   148\t                                    :operation canon-op\n   149\t                                    :outcome outcome})))))\n   150\t    @violations))\n   151\t\n   152\t\n   153\t(defn- run-descriptor-laws\n   154\t  \"Asserts descriptor projection laws on every handle:\n   155\t   - Handle satisfies IDaoStreamDescriptor regardless of surface.\n   156\t   - descriptor returns :dao.stream/ok.\n   157\t   - Sibling identity matches identity inside descriptor envelope.\n   158\t   - Descriptor envelope is valid.\"\n   159\t  [handle]\n   160\t  (let [violations (atom [])]\n   161\t    (if-not (v2/descriptor? handle)\n   162\t      (swap! violations conj {:check :handle-missing-descriptor-protocol})\n   163\t      (let [res (v2/descriptor handle)]\n   164\t        (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome res))\n   165\t          (swap! violations conj {:check :descriptor-outcome-not-ok :result res}))\n   166\t        (when-not (v2/descriptor-identity-consistent? res)\n   167\t          (swap! violations conj {:check :descriptor-identity-inconsistent :result res}))))\n   168\t    @violations))\n   169\t\n   170\t\n   171\t(defn- run-reader-laws\n   172\t  \"Asserts reader laws on a handle with reader surface:\n   173\t   - Handle satisfies IDaoStreamReader.\n   174\t   - Anchors :dao.stream/oldest and :dao.stream/newest mint valid cursors.\n   175\t   - Non-destructive reading: two cursors reading same position observe same value.\n   176\t   - Next returns valid outcomes and successor cursors.\"\n   177\t  [handle]\n   178\t  (let [violations (atom [])]\n   179\t    (if-not (v2/reader? handle)\n   180\t      (swap! violations conj {:check :handle-missing-reader-protocol})\n   181\t      (do\n   182\t        ;; Mint oldest and newest\n   183\t        (let [r-oldest (v2/cursor handle :dao.stream/oldest)]\n   184\t          (when-not (and (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r-oldest))\n   185\t                         (contains? r-oldest :dao.stream/cursor))\n   186\t            (swap! violations conj {:check :cursor-oldest-failed :result r-oldest})))\n   187\t        (let [r-newest (v2/cursor handle :dao.stream/newest)]\n   188\t          (when-not (and (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r-newest))\n   189\t                         (contains? r-newest :dao.stream/cursor))\n   190\t            (swap! violations conj {:check :cursor-newest-failed :result r-newest})))\n   190\t            (swap! violations conj {:check :cursor-newest-failed :result r-newest})))\n   191\t        ;; Non-destructive read test if elements present\n   192\t        (let [invalid-anchor (v2/cursor handle ::invalid-anchor)\n   193\t              _ (when-not (= :dao.stream/invalid-anchor\n   194\t                             (:dao.stream/outcome invalid-anchor))\n   195\t                  (swap! violations conj {:check :invalid-anchor-not-rejected\n   196\t                                          :result invalid-anchor}))\n   197\t              c0 (:dao.stream/cursor (v2/cursor handle :dao.stream/oldest))\n   198\t              res1 (v2/next handle c0)\n   199\t              res2 (v2/next handle c0)]\n   200\t          (when-not (= res1 res2)\n   201\t            (swap! violations conj {:check :destructive-read-detected\n   202\t                                    :first-read res1\n   203\t                                    :second-read res2}))\n   204\t          ;; The only legal way for a contract consumer to advance is to feed\n   205\t          ;; a stream-minted successor (or gap recovery cursor) back to next.\n   206\t          ;; Do not inspect cursor shape: it belongs to the transport.\n   207\t          (when (contains? #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/gap}\n   208\t                           (:dao.stream/outcome res1))\n   209\t            (let [successor (:dao.stream/cursor res1)\n   210\t                  follow-up (v2/next handle successor)]\n   211\t              (when-not (v2/valid-outcome? :next follow-up)\n   212\t                (swap! violations conj {:check :invalid-successor-or-recovery-cursor\n   213\t                                        :source res1 :follow-up follow-up})))))))\n   214\t    @violations))\n   215\t\n   216\t\n   217\t(defn- run-writer-laws\n   218\t  \"Asserts writer laws on a handle with writer surface:\n   219\t   - Handle satisfies IDaoStreamWriter.\n   220\t   - append! returns valid outcome.\"\n   221\t  [handle test-val]\n   222\t  (let [violations (atom [])]\n   223\t    (if-not (v2/writer? handle)\n   224\t      (swap! violations conj {:check :handle-missing-writer-protocol})\n   225\t      (let [res (v2/append! handle test-val)]\n   226\t        (when-not (v2/valid-outcome? :append! res)\n   227\t          (swap! violations conj {:check :invalid-append-outcome :result res}))))\n   228\t    @violations))\n   229\t\n   230\t\n   231\t(defn- run-close-laws\n   232\t  \"Asserts close laws on a handle with closable surface:\n   233\t   - Handle satisfies IDaoStreamClosable.\n   234\t   - close! returns :dao.stream/ok.\n   235\t   - close! is idempotent: subsequent close! returns :dao.stream/ok.\"\n   236\t  [handle]\n   237\t  (let [violations (atom [])]\n   238\t    (if-not (v2/closable? handle)\n   239\t      (swap! violations conj {:check :handle-missing-closable-protocol})\n   240\t      (let [res1 (v2/close! handle)\n   241\t            res2 (v2/close! handle)]\n   242\t        (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome res1))\n   243\t          (swap! violations conj {:check :close-outcome-not-ok :result res1}))\n   244\t        (when-not (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome res2))\n   245\t          (swap! violations conj {:check :close-idempotence-failed :result res2}))))\n   246\t    @violations))\n   247\t\n   248\t\n   249\t(defn run-conformance-suite\n   250\t  \"Executes the full declaration-driven conformance suite against manifest and optional handle.\n   251\t   Returns {:passed? true} or {:passed? false :failures [...]}.\"\n   252\t  ([manifest]\n   253\t   (run-conformance-suite manifest (when-let [hf (:handle-factory manifest)] (hf))))\n   254\t  ([manifest handle]\n   255\t   (let [failures (atom [])\n   256\t         manifest-res (validate-manifest manifest)\n   257\t         ;; Each destructive law block gets a fresh transport instance.  The\n   258\t         ;; manifest factory is the transport's responsibility; the supplied\n   259\t         ;; handle remains a compatibility fallback for older callers.\n   260\t         fresh-handle (fn []\n   261\t                        (if-let [factory (:handle-factory manifest)]\n   262\t                          (factory)\n   263\t                          handle))]\n   264\t     (if-not (:valid? manifest-res)\n   265\t       {:passed? false :failures (:errors manifest-res)}\n   266\t       (do\n   267\t         ;; 1. Induction & exhaustiveness check\n   268\t         (let [ind-v (run-induction-coverage manifest)]\n   269\t           (when (seq ind-v)\n   270\t             (swap! failures concat ind-v)))\n   271\t\n   272\t         ;; 2. Descriptor identity laws (always checked)\n   273\t         (when (fresh-handle)\n   274\t           (let [desc-v (run-descriptor-laws (fresh-handle))]\n   275\t             (when (seq desc-v)\n   276\t               (swap! failures concat desc-v)))\n   277\t\n   278\t           ;; 3. Reader laws (only if reader declared)\n   279\t           (when (contains? (:surfaces manifest) :reader)\n   280\t             (let [r-v (run-reader-laws (fresh-handle))]\n   281\t               (when (seq r-v)\n   282\t                 (swap! failures concat r-v))))\n   283\t\n   284\t           ;; 4. Writer laws (only if writer declared)\n   285\t           (when (contains? (:surfaces manifest) :writer)\n   286\t             (let [w-v (run-writer-laws (fresh-handle) ::conformance-token)]\n   287\t               (when (seq w-v)\n   288\t                 (swap! failures concat w-v))))\n   289\t\n   290\t           ;; 5. Close laws (only if closable declared)\n   291\t           (when (contains? (:surfaces manifest) :closable)\n   292\t             (let [c-v (run-close-laws (fresh-handle))]\n   293\t               (when (seq c-v)\n   294\t                 (swap! failures concat c-v)))))\n   295\t\n   296\t         (if (empty? @failures)\n   297\t           {:passed? true}\n   298\t           {:passed? false :failures @failures}))))))\n   299\t\n   300\t\n   301\t;; =============================================================================\n   302\t;; Concurrency Oracle (Bounded Offline Linearizability Checker)\n   303\t;; =============================================================================\n   304\t\n   305\t(defn record-op!\n   306\t  \"Helper to record an operation invocation interval into a history atom.\n   307\t   Returns the result of executing (f).\"\n   308\t  [history-atom clock-atom op args f]\n   309\t  (let [start (swap! clock-atom inc)\n   310\t        result (f)\n   311\t        finish (swap! clock-atom inc)]\n   312\t    (swap! history-atom conj {:id (count @history-atom)\n   313\t                              :op op\n   314\t                              :args args\n   315\t                              :result result\n   316\t                              :start start\n   317\t                              :end finish})\n   318\t    result))\n   319\t\n   320\t\n   321\t(defn- happens-before?\n   322\t  \"True if op A completed before op B was invoked (A ≺ B).\"\n   323\t  [op-a op-b]\n   324\t  (< (:end op-a) (:start op-b)))\n   325\t\n   326\t\n   327\t(defn- legal-next-ops\n   328\t  \"Given remaining operations to schedule and already scheduled operations,\n   329\t   returns operations from remaining that have no un-scheduled predecessors\n   330\t   in the happens-before partial order.\"\n   331\t  [remaining scheduled]\n   332\t  (filter (fn [candidate]\n   333\t            (not-any? (fn [other]\n   334\t                        (and (not (contains? scheduled other))\n   335\t                             (happens-before? other candidate)))\n   336\t                      remaining))\n   337\t          remaining))\n   338\t\n   339\t\n   340\t(defn check-linearizability\n   341\t  \"Offline bounded linearizability checker.\n   342\t   history: vector of {:id .. :op .. :args .. :result .. :start .. :end ..}\n   343\t   initial-model: abstract sequential state\n   344\t   step-fn: (fn [model-state op args result]) -> next-state or nil if invalid transition.\n   345\t\n   346\t   Returns {:linearizable? true :linearization [...]} if a valid sequential\n   347\t   interleaving respecting happens-before exists, or {:linearizable? false}.\"\n   348\t  [history initial-model step-fn]\n   349\t  (let [total (count history)]\n   350\t    (letfn [(search\n   351\t              [current-model remaining scheduled-set acc]\n   352\t              (if (empty? remaining)\n   353\t                {:linearizable? true :linearization acc}\n   354\t                (let [candidates (legal-next-ops remaining scheduled-set)]\n   355\t                  (loop [cands candidates]\n   356\t                    (if (empty? cands)\n   357\t                      nil\n   358\t                      (let [candidate (first cands)\n   359\t                            next-model (step-fn current-model\n   360\t                                                (:op candidate)\n   361\t                                                (:args candidate)\n   362\t                                                (:result candidate))]\n   363\t                        (if next-model\n   364\t                          (let [sub (search next-model\n   365\t                                            (disj remaining candidate)\n   366\t                                            (conj scheduled-set candidate)\n   367\t                                            (conj acc candidate))]\n   368\t                            (if (:linearizable? sub)\n   369\t                              sub\n   370\t                              (recur (rest cands))))\n   370\t                              (recur (rest cands))))\n   371\t                          (recur (rest cands)))))))))]\n   372\t      (or (search initial-model (set history) #{} [])\n   373\t          {:linearizable? false\n   374\t           :total total\n   375\t           :reason :no-legal-linearization}))))\n   376\t\n   377\t\n   378\t;; =============================================================================\n   379\t;; Pure Abstract Model for Stream & Ring Buffer Linearizability\n   380\t;; =============================================================================\n   381\t\n   382\t(defn make-abstract-stream-model\n   383\t  \"Creates the pure abstract sequential model for a stream/ringbuffer:\n   384\t   - stream-identity: logical-stream identity\n   385\t   - capacity: nil (unbounded) or integer (evict-oldest bound)\n   386\t   - initial-elements: optional initial sequence of values\"\n   387\t  ([stream-identity]\n   388\t   (make-abstract-stream-model stream-identity nil []))\n   389\t  ([stream-identity capacity]\n   390\t   (make-abstract-stream-model stream-identity capacity []))\n   391\t  ([stream-identity capacity initial-elements]\n   392\t   (make-abstract-stream-model stream-identity capacity initial-elements {}))\n   393\t  ([stream-identity capacity initial-elements options]\n   394\t   {:stream-identity stream-identity\n   395\t    :capacity capacity\n   396\t    :elements (vec (map-indexed (fn [i v] {:pos i :val v}) initial-elements))\n   397\t    :next-pos (count initial-elements)\n   398\t    :closed? false\n   399\t    ;; Attachment entries are supplied by the test composition.  A close on an\n   400\t    ;; attachment freezes its own visible tail, not the logical stream.\n   401\t    :attachments (or (:attachments options) {})\n   402\t    ;; Cursor layout is transport-owned.  The model receives projection\n   403\t    ;; functions rather than depending on a particular map key convention.\n   404\t    :cursor-projector\n   405\t    (or (:cursor-projector options)\n   406\t        {:identity (fn [cursor] (:stream-identity cursor))\n   407\t         :position (fn [cursor] (:position cursor))})}))\n   408\t\n   409\t\n   410\t(defn- invocation\n   411\t  \"Normalizes legacy positional arguments and the extensible oracle argument\n   412\t   shape.  New histories may use\n   413\t   {:dao.stream/args [...] :dao.stream/handle {:attachment <id>}}; a missing\n   414\t   handle means the logical-stream owner.\"\n   415\t  [args]\n   416\t  (if (and (map? args) (contains? args :dao.stream/args))\n   417\t    args\n   418\t    {:dao.stream/args args :dao.stream/handle {:owner? true}}))\n   419\t\n   420\t\n   421\t(defn- attachment-id\n   422\t  [inv]\n   423\t  (get-in inv [:dao.stream/handle :attachment]))\n   424\t\n   425\t\n   426\t(defn- attachment-state\n   427\t  [state inv]\n   428\t  (when-let [id (attachment-id inv)]\n   429\t    (get-in state [:attachments id])))\n   430\t\n   431\t\n   432\t(defn- visible-tail\n   433\t  [state inv]\n   434\t  (or (:tail (attachment-state state inv)) (:next-pos state)))\n   435\t\n   436\t\n   437\t(defn- cursor-identity\n   438\t  [state cursor]\n   439\t  (when (map? cursor)\n   440\t    ((get-in state [:cursor-projector :identity]) cursor)))\n   441\t\n   442\t\n   443\t(defn- cursor-position\n   444\t  [state cursor]\n   445\t  (when (map? cursor)\n   446\t    ((get-in state [:cursor-projector :position]) cursor)))\n   447\t\n   448\t\n   449\t(defn- result-cursor-position\n   450\t  [state result]\n   451\t  (cursor-position state (:dao.stream/cursor result)))\n   452\t\n   453\t\n   454\t(defn- retained-first\n   455\t  [state]\n   456\t  (or (:pos (first (:elements state))) (:next-pos state)))\n   457\t\n   458\t\n   459\t(defn abstract-stream-step\n   460\t  \"Pure abstract transition function for stream operations:\n   461\t   state: abstract model state\n   462\t   op: :append!, :close!, :cursor, or :next\n   463\t   args: arguments passed to operation\n   464\t   result: observed outcome map from operation\n   465\t   Returns next state if the operation and outcome are legally accepted by\n   466\t   the abstract sequential model; returns nil if transition is invalid.\"\n   467\t  [state op args result]\n   468\t  (let [outcome (:dao.stream/outcome result)\n   469\t        inv (invocation args)\n   470\t        positional (:dao.stream/args inv)\n   471\t        attachment (attachment-state state inv)\n   472\t        handle-closed? (and attachment (not (:open? attachment)))]\n   473\t    (case op\n   474\t      :append!\n   475\t      (let [[_val] positional]\n   476\t        (if (or (:closed? state) handle-closed?)\n   477\t          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/closed) state)\n   478\t          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/ok)\n   479\t            (let [pos (:next-pos state)\n   480\t                  new-elem {:pos pos :val (first positional)}\n   481\t                  new-elements (conj (:elements state) new-elem)\n   482\t                  trimmed-elements (if (and (:capacity state)\n   483\t                                            (> (count new-elements) (:capacity state)))\n   484\t                                     (subvec new-elements (- (count new-elements) (:capacity state)))\n   485\t                                     new-elements)]\n   486\t              (assoc state\n   487\t                     :elements trimmed-elements\n   488\t                     :next-pos (inc pos))))))\n   489\t\n   490\t      :close!\n   491\t      (when (= outcome :dao.stream/ok)\n   492\t        (if-let [id (attachment-id inv)]\n   493\t          (if (get-in state [:attachments id])\n   494\t            (if (get-in state [:attachments id :open?])\n   495\t              (assoc-in state [:attachments id]\n   496\t                        {:open? false :tail (:next-pos state)})\n   497\t              state)\n   498\t            nil)\n   499\t          (assoc state :closed? true)))\n   500\t\n   501\t      :cursor\n   502\t      (let [[anchor] positional]\n   503\t        (if handle-closed?\n   504\t          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/closed) state)\n   505\t          (case anchor\n   506\t            :dao.stream/oldest\n   507\t            (when (and (= outcome :dao.stream/ok)\n   508\t                       (map? (:dao.stream/cursor result)))\n   509\t              (let [expected-pos (min (retained-first state) (visible-tail state inv))]\n   510\t                (when (= (result-cursor-position state result) expected-pos) state)))\n   511\t\n   512\t            :dao.stream/newest\n   513\t            (when (and (= outcome :dao.stream/ok)\n   514\t                       (map? (:dao.stream/cursor result)))\n   515\t              (when (= (result-cursor-position state result) (visible-tail state inv)) state))\n   516\t\n   517\t            ;; Invalid anchor\n   518\t            (when (= outcome :dao.stream/invalid-anchor)\n   519\t              state))))\n   520\t\n   521\t      :next\n   522\t      (let [[cur] positional]\n   523\t        (cond\n   524\t          (not (map? cur))\n   525\t          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/invalid-cursor) state)\n   526\t\n   527\t          (nil? (cursor-identity state cur))\n   528\t          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/invalid-cursor) state)\n   529\t\n   530\t          (not= (:stream-identity state) (cursor-identity state cur))\n   531\t          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch) state)\n   532\t\n   533\t          (not (integer? (cursor-position state cur)))\n   534\t          (when (= outcome :dao.stream/invalid-cursor) state)\n   535\t\n   536\t          :else\n   537\t          (let [pos (cursor-position state cur)\n   538\t                first-pos (retained-first state)\n   539\t                tail (visible-tail state inv)]\n   540\t            (cond\n   541\t              ;; Evicted position -> gap. Recovery names the earliest retained\n   542\t              ;; position, even when a closed attachment's frozen tail is gone.\n   543\t              (< pos first-pos)\n   544\t              (when (and (= outcome :dao.stream/gap)\n   545\t                         (= first-pos (result-cursor-position state result)))\n   546\t                state)\n   547\t\n   548\t              ;; Retained position visible through this handle -> ok.\n   549\t              (< pos tail)\n   550\t              (let [elem (first (filter #(= (:pos %) pos) (:elements state)))]\n   551\t                (when (and elem (= outcome :dao.stream/ok)\n   552\t                           (= (:val elem) (:dao.stream/value result))\n   553\t                           (= (inc pos) (result-cursor-position state result)))\n   554\t                  state))\n   555\t\n   556\t              ;; A frozen attachment is at end even while the logical stream\n   557\t              ;; remains open.  Otherwise logical close determines end/blocked.\n   558\t              :else\n   559\t              (if (or handle-closed? (:closed? state))\n   560\t                (when (= outcome :dao.stream/end) state)\n   561\t                (when (= outcome :dao.stream/blocked) state))))))\n   562\t\n   563\t      ;; Unknown op\n   564\t      nil)))\n     1\t(ns dao.stream.ringbuffer-test\n     2\t  (:require [clojure.test :refer [deftest is testing]]\n     3\t            [dao.stream :as stream]\n     4\t            [dao.stream.conformance :as conformance]\n     5\t            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ring]))\n     6\t\n     7\t\n     8\t(def spec\n     9\t  {:dao.stream/type ring/transport-type\n    10\t   :dao.stream.ringbuffer/capacity 2})\n    11\t\n    12\t\n    13\t(defn handle\n    14\t  []\n    15\t  (:dao.stream/handle (ring/create! spec)))\n    16\t\n    17\t\n    18\t(defn cur\n    19\t  [h a]\n    20\t  (:dao.stream/cursor (stream/cursor h a)))\n    21\t\n    22\t\n    23\t(deftest retention-and-gaps\n    24\t  (let [h (handle) c (cur h :dao.stream/oldest)]\n    25\t    (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/append! h :a))))\n    26\t    (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/append! h :b))))\n    27\t    (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/append! h :c))))\n    28\t    (is (= :dao.stream/gap (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/next h c))))\n    29\t    (let [r (stream/next h (:dao.stream/cursor (stream/next h c)))]\n    30\t      (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome r)))\n    31\t      (is (= :b (:dao.stream/value r))))))\n    32\t\n    33\t\n    34\t(deftest anchors-and-identity\n    35\t  (let [h (handle) other (handle)\n    36\t        newest (cur h :dao.stream/newest)]\n    37\t    (is (= :dao.stream/blocked (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/next h newest))))\n    38\t    (is (= :dao.stream/invalid-anchor\n    39\t           (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/cursor h :bad))))\n    40\t    (is (= :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch\n    41\t           (:dao.stream/outcome\n    42\t             (stream/next other newest))))))\n    43\t\n    44\t\n    45\t(deftest close-freezes-attachment\n    46\t  (let [owner (handle)\n    47\t        d (:dao.stream/descriptor (stream/descriptor owner))\n    48\t        attached (:dao.stream/handle\n    49\t                   ((ring/make-attacher {(:dao.stream/identity d) owner}) d))\n    50\t        c (cur attached :dao.stream/newest)]\n    51\t    (stream/append! owner :before)\n    52\t    (stream/close! attached)\n    53\t    (is (= :dao.stream/closed (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/append! attached :x))))\n    54\t    (stream/append! owner :after)\n    55\t    (let [before (stream/next attached c)]\n    56\t      (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome before)))\n    57\t      (is (= :before (:dao.stream/value before)))\n    58\t      (is (= :dao.stream/end\n    59\t             (:dao.stream/outcome\n    60\t               (stream/next attached (:dao.stream/cursor before))))))\n    61\t    (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/append! owner :ok))))))\n    62\t\n    63\t\n    64\t(deftest attachment-resolution\n    65\t  (let [h (handle) d (:dao.stream/descriptor (stream/descriptor h))\n    66\t        resolver {(:dao.stream/identity d) h}]\n    67\t    (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome (ring/attach! resolver d))))\n    68\t    (is (= :dao.stream/not-found\n    69\t           (:dao.stream/outcome (ring/attach! {} d))))\n    70\t    (is (= :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor\n    71\t           (:dao.stream/outcome (ring/attach! resolver {:dao.stream/type :other}))))))\n    72\t\n    73\t\n    74\t(defn- attachment\n    75\t  ([] (attachment spec))\n    76\t  ([creation-spec]\n    77\t   (let [owner (:dao.stream/handle (ring/create! creation-spec))\n    78\t         descriptor (:dao.stream/descriptor (stream/descriptor owner))]\n    79\t     {:owner owner\n    80\t      :descriptor descriptor\n    81\t      :attached (:dao.stream/handle\n    82\t                  (ring/attach! {(:dao.stream/identity descriptor) owner}\n    83\t                                descriptor))})))\n    84\t\n    85\t\n    86\t(def ringbuffer-manifest\n    87\t  \"The reference transport's complete outcome declaration.  Every fixture\n    88\t   operates a fresh real handle; it never fabricates an outcome map.\"\n    89\t  {:dao.stream/type ring/transport-type\n    90\t   :surfaces #{:reader :writer :closable}\n    91\t   :handle-factory handle\n    92\t   :operations\n    93\t   {:create! {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/invalid-spec}\n    94\t              :exclusions {:dao.stream/not-found \"host dispatch selects transport\"\n    95\t                           :dao.stream/transport-error \"in-memory allocation has no operational failure channel\"}}\n    96\t    :attach! {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor :dao.stream/not-found}\n    97\t              :exclusions {:dao.stream/transport-error \"captured in-memory resolver has no failure channel\"}}\n    98\t    :descriptor {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok} :exclusions {}}\n    99\t    :cursor {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/invalid-anchor :dao.stream/closed}\n   100\t             :exclusions {:dao.stream/transport-error \"one in-memory state deref cannot fail operationally\"}}\n   101\t    :next {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/blocked :dao.stream/end\n   102\t                       :dao.stream/gap :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch :dao.stream/invalid-cursor}\n   103\t           :exclusions {:dao.stream/transport-error \"coherent in-memory state has no failure channel\"}}\n   104\t    :append! {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok :dao.stream/closed}\n   105\t              :exclusions {:dao.stream/full \"evict-oldest retention never refuses append\"\n   106\t                           :dao.stream/invalid-value \"reference transport accepts all host values\"\n   107\t                           :dao.stream/transport-error \"coherent in-memory state has no failure channel\"}}\n   108\t    :close! {:produces #{:dao.stream/ok} :exclusions {}}}\n   109\t   :fixtures\n   110\t   {:create! {:dao.stream/ok #(ring/create! spec)\n   111\t              :dao.stream/invalid-spec #(ring/create! {:dao.stream/type ring/transport-type})}\n   112\t    :attach! {:dao.stream/ok #(let [{:keys [owner descriptor]} (attachment)]\n   113\t                                (ring/attach! {(:dao.stream/identity descriptor) owner} descriptor))\n   114\t              :dao.stream/invalid-descriptor #(ring/attach! {} {:dao.stream/type :other})\n   115\t              :dao.stream/not-found #(let [h (handle)\n   116\t                                           d (:dao.stream/descriptor (stream/descriptor h))]\n   117\t                                       (ring/attach! {} d))}\n   118\t    :descriptor {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/descriptor (handle))}\n   119\t    :cursor {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/cursor (handle) :dao.stream/oldest)\n   120\t             :dao.stream/invalid-anchor #(stream/cursor (handle) ::invalid-anchor)\n   121\t             :dao.stream/closed #(let [{:keys [attached]} (attachment)]\n   122\t                                   (stream/close! attached)\n   123\t                                   (stream/cursor attached :dao.stream/oldest))}\n   124\t    :next {:dao.stream/ok #(let [h (handle)\n   125\t                                 c (cur h :dao.stream/oldest)]\n   126\t                             (stream/append! h :value)\n   127\t                             (stream/next h c))\n   128\t           :dao.stream/blocked #(let [h (handle)]\n   129\t                                  (stream/next h (cur h :dao.stream/newest)))\n   130\t           :dao.stream/end #(let [h (handle)\n   131\t                                  c (cur h :dao.stream/newest)]\n   132\t                              (stream/close! h)\n   133\t                              (stream/next h c))\n   134\t           :dao.stream/gap #(let [h (:dao.stream/handle (ring/create! (assoc spec ring/capacity-key 1)))\n   135\t                                  c (cur h :dao.stream/oldest)]\n   136\t                              (stream/append! h :old)\n   137\t                              (stream/append! h :new)\n   138\t                              (stream/next h c))\n   139\t           :dao.stream/cursor-mismatch #(let [left (handle) right (handle)]\n   140\t                                          (stream/next right (cur left :dao.stream/oldest)))\n   141\t           :dao.stream/invalid-cursor #(stream/next (handle) nil)}\n   142\t    :append! {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/append! (handle) :value)\n   143\t              :dao.stream/closed #(let [h (handle)]\n   144\t                                    (stream/close! h)\n   145\t                                    (stream/append! h :value))}\n   146\t    :close! {:dao.stream/ok #(stream/close! (handle))}}})\n   147\t\n   148\t\n   149\t(deftest ringbuffer-conformance-test\n   150\t  (let [result (conformance/run-conformance-suite ringbuffer-manifest)]\n   151\t    (is (:passed? result) (str \"Conformance failures: \" (:failures result)))))\n   152\t\n   153\t\n   154\t(deftest attachment-lifecycle-regressions\n   155\t  (testing \"attachment close is idempotent and freezes its original tail\"\n   156\t    (let [{:keys [owner attached]} (attachment (assoc spec ring/capacity-key 4))\n   157\t          cursor (cur attached :dao.stream/newest)]\n   158\t      (stream/append! owner :before)\n   159\t      (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/close! attached))))\n   160\t      (stream/append! owner :after-first-close)\n   161\t      (is (= :dao.stream/ok (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/close! attached))))\n   162\t      (stream/append! owner :after-second-close)\n   163\t      (let [seen (stream/next attached cursor)]\n   164\t        (is (= :before (:dao.stream/value seen)))\n   165\t        (is (= :dao.stream/end\n   166\t               (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/next attached (:dao.stream/cursor seen))))))))\n   167\t\n   168\t  (testing \"gap recovery on a frozen attachment never points past its tail\"\n   169\t    (let [owner (:dao.stream/handle (ring/create! (assoc spec ring/capacity-key 1)))\n   170\t          descriptor (:dao.stream/descriptor (stream/descriptor owner))\n   171\t          attached (:dao.stream/handle (ring/attach! {(:dao.stream/identity descriptor) owner} descriptor))\n   172\t          cursor (cur attached :dao.stream/oldest)]\n   173\t      (stream/append! owner :before)\n   174\t      (stream/close! attached)\n   175\t      (stream/append! owner :after)\n   176\t      (let [gap (stream/next attached cursor)]\n   177\t        (is (= :dao.stream/gap (:dao.stream/outcome gap)))\n   178\t        (is (= :dao.stream/end\n   179\t               (:dao.stream/outcome (stream/next attached (:dao.stream/cursor gap))))))))\n   180\t\n   230\t\n   231\t(def ^:private ring-projector\n   232\t  {:cursor-projector {:identity :dao.stream.ringbuffer/identity\n   233\t                      :position :dao.stream.ringbuffer/position}})\n   234\t\n   235\t\n   236\t(defn- ring-model\n   237\t  \"The abstract model for one ring buffer, told which attachments exist.\n   238\t   Attachment entries are composition facts the model cannot observe.\"\n   239\t  ([stream-identity capacity]\n   240\t   (ring-model stream-identity capacity {}))\n   241\t  ([stream-identity capacity attachments]\n   242\t   (conformance/make-abstract-stream-model\n   243\t     stream-identity capacity []\n   244\t     (assoc ring-projector :attachments attachments))))\n   245\t\n   246\t\n   247\t(defn- linearizes?\n   248\t  [history model]\n   249\t  (conformance/check-linearizability history model conformance/abstract-stream-step))\n   250\t\n   251\t\n   252\t#?(:cljd nil\n   253\t   :clj\n   254\t   (defn- on-attachment\n   255\t     \"The oracle's invocation shape for an operation issued through an attachment\n   256\t      handle rather than the logical-stream owner.\"\n   257\t     [attachment args]\n   258\t     {:dao.stream/args args :dao.stream/handle {:attachment attachment}}))\n   259\t\n   260\t\n   261\t(deftest linearizability-oracle-rejects-an-invalid-history\n   262\t  ;; The oracle is only evidence if it can fail.  Here `append!` completed\n   263\t  ;; before `next` was invoked, so every legal linearization must observe the\n   264\t  ;; appended value; a `blocked` read is a real ordering violation.\n   265\t  (let [h (handle)\n   266\t        stream-identity (:dao.stream/identity (stream/descriptor h))\n   267\t        c (cur h :dao.stream/oldest)\n   268\t        history [{:id 0 :op :append! :args [:a]\n   269\t                  :result {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/ok} :start 1 :end 2}\n   270\t                 {:id 1 :op :next :args [c]\n   271\t                  :result {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/blocked} :start 3 :end 4}]\n   272\t        result (linearizes? history (ring-model stream-identity 2))]\n   273\t    (is (false? (:linearizable? result)))\n   274\t    (is (= :no-legal-linearization (:reason result)))))\n   275\t\n   276\t\n   277\t#?(:cljd nil\n   278\t   :clj\n   279\t   (defn- run-concurrently\n   280\t     \"Run each thunk on its own thread against one shared history and clock.\n   281\t      Every thunk receives `[history clock]` and records its own invocation\n   282\t      interval with `conformance/record-op!`.  A caller may supply a history\n   283\t      already holding earlier, strictly ordered operations.\"\n   284\t     ([thunks] (run-concurrently (atom []) (atom 0) thunks))\n   285\t     ([history clock thunks]\n   286\t      (let [start (promise)\n   287\t            threads (mapv (fn [thunk]\n   288\t                            (future (deref start 5000 :go)\n   289\t                                    (thunk history clock)))\n   290\t                          thunks)]\n   291\t        (deliver start :go)\n   292\t        (doseq [t threads]\n   293\t          (is (not= :timeout (deref t 5000 :timeout)) \"concurrent operation timed out\"))\n   294\t        @history))))\n   295\t\n   296\t\n   297\t#?(:cljd nil\n   298\t   :clj\n   299\t   (deftest bounded-concurrent-linearizability-histories\n   300\t     (testing \"concurrent append/append\"\n   301\t       (let [h (handle)\n   302\t             stream-identity (:dao.stream/identity (stream/descriptor h))\n   303\t             history (run-concurrently\n   304\t                       [(fn [hist clock]\n   305\t                          (conformance/record-op! hist clock :append! [:a]\n   306\t                                                  #(stream/append! h :a)))\n   307\t                        (fn [hist clock]\n   308\t                          (conformance/record-op! hist clock :append! [:b]\n   309\t                                                  #(stream/append! h :b)))])\n   310\t             result (linearizes? history (ring-model stream-identity 2))]\n   311\t         (is (= 2 (count history)))\n   312\t         (is (:linearizable? result) (str history \" \" result))))\n   313\t\n   314\t     (testing \"concurrent append/next\"\n   315\t       (let [h (handle)\n   316\t             stream-identity (:dao.stream/identity (stream/descriptor h))\n   317\t             c (cur h :dao.stream/oldest)\n   318\t             history (run-concurrently\n   319\t                       [(fn [hist clock]\n   320\t                          (conformance/record-op! hist clock :append! [:a]\n   321\t                                                  #(stream/append! h :a)))\n   322\t                        (fn [hist clock]\n   323\t                          (conformance/record-op! hist clock :next [c]\n   324\t                                                  #(stream/next h c)))])\n   325\t             result (linearizes? history (ring-model stream-identity 2))]\n   326\t         (is (:linearizable? result) (str history \" \" result))))\n   327\t\n   328\t     (testing \"concurrent append/close\"\n   329\t       (let [h (handle)\n   330\t             stream-identity (:dao.stream/identity (stream/descriptor h))\n   331\t             history (run-concurrently\n   332\t                       [(fn [hist clock]\n   333\t                          (conformance/record-op! hist clock :append! [:a]\n   334\t                                                  #(stream/append! h :a)))\n   335\t                        (fn [hist clock]\n   336\t                          (conformance/record-op! hist clock :close! []\n   337\t                                                  #(stream/close! h)))])\n   338\t             result (linearizes? history (ring-model stream-identity 2))]\n   339\t         (is (:linearizable? result) (str history \" \" result))))\n   340\t\n    96\t## Result Convention\n    97\t\n    98\tEvery DaoStream operation returns data.\n    99\t\n   100\t- Each operation's set of outcomes is **exhaustive**: a transport may produce\n   101\t  fewer of them, never any outside the set. Which subset it produces is part of\n   102\t  its declared nature (see Surfaces). An outcome is a map whose\n   103\t  `:dao.stream/outcome` key names the outcome with a qualified keyword. Every\n   104\t  operation returns such a map — including projections like `descriptor` and\n   105\t  `cursor`, whose success map carries the projected value.\n   106\t- `:dao.stream/outcome` is present in every result map and is never repeated\n   107\t  in the per-outcome tables below; the keys those tables name are required in\n   108\t  addition to it.\n   109\t- Result maps are **open**: the contract fixes which keys are required per\n   110\t  outcome, and a consumer must ignore keys it does not understand. This is\n   111\t  what allows future extensions (for example readiness notification) to be\n   112\t  added without breaking existing consumers.\n   113\t- Exceptions are reserved for defects in the host's own assembly of\n   114\t  DaoStream, detected before any operation runs. Everything observable at an\n   115\t  operation returns data: closed, full, blocked, end, gap, not found,\n   116\t  malformed input, and transport defects are all outcomes, never exceptions\n   117\t  and never callbacks.\n   118\t- All qualified keywords in results live under `:dao.stream/…`.\n   119\t\n   120\t## The IO Model\n   225\treceiver's fixed contract already declares the surface. But a handle does not\n   226\tsurvive encoding, and any\n   227\ttransport that encodes its elements — a file as much as a network socket —\n   228\tis a serialization boundary. Descriptors exist for crossing those\n   229\tboundaries, where the live value cannot travel and a name must. (What a\n   230\ttransport can carry is transport-owned: handing an encoding transport a\n   231\tvalue it cannot encode is reported as data like any other outcome.)\n   232\t`attach!` therefore consumes only descriptors, and\n   233\tresolves them against the transport's inherent operational state — the\n   234\tstreams a network endpoint serves because serving them is what it is, the\n   235\tfiles a filesystem holds. There is no stream table in this contract: no\n   236\tregistry maps descriptors to live handles, so a descriptor arriving\n   237\tsomewhere with nothing behind it — a ring buffer's descriptor on any other\n   238\thost, or on its own host absent a directory some composition chose to keep —\n   239\thonestly resolves to `:dao.stream/not-found`.\n   240\t\n   241\t### Envelopes\n   242\t\n   243\tA **creation specification** is a map with one required key:\n   244\t\n   245\t- `:dao.stream/type` — a qualified keyword naming the transport. This is the\n   383\t## Surfaces\n   384\t\n   385\tA handle implements the subset of the public surface its transport can\n   386\thonor — **reader** (`cursor`, `next`), **writer** (`append!`), **closable**\n   387\t(`close!`) — and the transport declares which. The Cursors, Reading, and\n   388\tRetention and Gaps sections govern handles with a reader surface only.\n   389\t\n   390\t`descriptor` belongs to no surface: every handle answers it with both\n   391\treachability and logical-stream identity, because both outlive any attachment.\n   392\tAn operation a handle does not\n   393\tdeclare is not part of that handle at all: there is nothing to call, so there\n   394\tis no operation for this contract to give an outcome. What a host does when\n   395\tasked for a method that is not there is the host's, and no transport may\n   396\tanswer such a call with a DaoStream outcome map — an outcome would imply the\n   397\toperation exists.\n   398\t\n   399\tA transport may also offer operations of its own beside the public surface —\n   400\tanswering a question this contract does not ask, such as how far a cursor\n   401\ttrails the newest value. They are transport-owned, they are not DaoStream\n   402\toperations, and nothing here defines or constrains them. **Code written\n   403\tagainst this contract may not reach for one**: it must work on any transport\n   404\tdeclaring the surfaces it uses, and a transport-owned operation is by\n   405\tdefinition absent from some of them.\n   406\t\n   407\tAn interpreter may still use one, on the same terms as any other thing a\n   408\tcomposition supplies. What it may not do is *find* it — reach through a handle\n   409\tfor an operation the contract never promised, or dispatch on which transport\n   410\tit was given. It receives the operation as an ordinary argument from the\n   411\tcomposition that wired it, exactly as it receives the streams it reads and the\n   412\tpolicies it applies, and it then depends on a function it was handed rather\n   413\tthan on a transport it detected. Such an interpreter is composed for a\n   414\tparticular medium and says so; it is not contract-generic code, and nothing\n   415\tcontract-generic may be built on it.\n   416\t\n   417\tThe reader surface is a promise: the handle presents its elements as\n   418\tone positioned, append-only, retained sequence that cursors can observe and\n   419\tre-observe. A transport whose medium retains nothing cannot make that\n   420\tpromise and has no reader surface: its inbound events are deposited by the\n   421\thost's adapter onto the stream the host composition wired for that boundary\n   422\t— where the reading model applies in full. The log lives beside such a\n   423\ttransport, composed explicitly by the host, never hidden inside the\n   424\ttransport. A deposit destination must admit every valid boundary event per\n   425\tits declared retention; wiring one that can refuse is a host assembly\n   426\tdefect. Deposit admission is declared, never interrogated: the contract's\n   427\tpublic surface has no way to ask a handle its retention policy. A constructor\n   428\ttakes the deposit destination and its admission declaration as data. This is\n   429\tconfiguration provenance, not runtime introspection. (`dao.stream.ws.md`\n   430\tspecifies one such transport and the admission rule in detail.)\n   431\t\n   432\tA boundary that can no longer deposit must make itself **observably gone**. Its\n   433\tone permitted action is closing the host resource it itself holds; it invokes\n   434\tnothing above itself, retries nothing, and interprets nothing. This is the sole\n   435\taction a boundary adapter takes beyond appending, and it is not a judgement\n   436\tabout any event — it is the same action for every cause. One corner is\n   437\tirreducible: when the deposit that failed was the boundary's only channel, the\n   438\trecord of its own departure has nowhere to land, and the failure is observable\n   439\tonly to whoever holds the deposit result and through subsequent operations on\n   440\tthe handle.\n   441\t\n   442\tA transport also declares **which outcomes it produces**, and for each one it\n   443\texcludes, why. There are three honest reasons: the outcome's precondition is\n   444\timpossible by the transport's nature (an unbounded log never evicts, so never\n   445\treports `gap`); another policy answers the same condition, named (a ring buffer\n   446\tevicts rather than refusing, so pressure surfaces as a `gap` to a cursor that\n   447\tspans the eviction, and never as `full`); or the answer is displaced to another channel, named (a transport\n   448\tthat cannot know a remote answer at call time reports it as data elsewhere). An\n   449\texclusion with no stated reason is an unimplemented outcome, not a declaration.\n   450\t\n   451\t### Where the asynchrony goes\n   471\tA cursor is an immutable value owned by the interpreter that holds it.\n   472\t\n   473\t- Cursors are minted by the stream: `cursor` on a handle takes an **anchor**\n   474\t  and returns `:dao.stream/ok` with `:dao.stream/cursor`. The contract\n   475\t  defines two anchors — `:dao.stream/oldest`, the earliest retained\n   476\t  position, and `:dao.stream/newest`, positioned after the newest appended\n   477\t  value so the first `next` observes the next value to arrive. A transport\n   478\t  whose medium is naturally addressable may accept additional\n   479\t  transport-owned anchors (a byte offset, a timestamp). An anchor that is\n   480\t  malformed or not honored by this transport yields\n   481\t  `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`. On a handle whose attachment has closed,\n   482\t  `cursor` yields `:dao.stream/closed`; a handle on the logical stream\n   483\t  itself keeps minting cursors after close, so retained history remains\n   484\t  readable. Minting may have to consult the medium — locating the earliest\n   485\t  retained position on a durable log is itself a read — so where a\n   486\t  transport's reads can fail at runtime, minting can too, and `cursor`\n   487\t  yields `:dao.stream/transport-error` for it; a transport whose medium\n   488\t  cannot fail this way excludes the outcome like any other (see Surfaces).\n   489\t  `cursor`'s outcome set is therefore `:dao.stream/ok` (with\n   490\t  `:dao.stream/cursor`), `:dao.stream/invalid-anchor`,\n   491\t  `:dao.stream/closed`, and `:dao.stream/transport-error`.\n   492\t- A composition that intends to observe events caused by an operation mints\n   493\t  its `:dao.stream/newest` cursor **before** invoking that operation. Minting\n   494\t  after `attach!`, endpoint bind, handoff installation, or any other operation\n   495\t  that can deposit would intentionally skip events deposited in between.\n   496\t- **A freshly minted `:dao.stream/oldest` is a position, not a claim about\n   497\t  completeness.** The anchor names the earliest *retained* position, which on\n   498\t  an evicting transport advances as values are lost. A `gap` is reported to a\n   499\t  cursor that spans an eviction; a cursor minted after one is a valid cursor\n   500\t  onto the surviving suffix and reports nothing, because nothing is wrong with\n   501\t  the cursor. Retained history is not complete history, and no anchor can make\n   502\t  it so. A consumer that requires complete history gets it from the transport's\n   503\t  declared retention (see *Retention and Gaps*), never from an anchor.\n   504\t- A composition on a transport that **can evict**, intending to detect whether\n   505\t  history was lost, mints an **origin cursor** — `:dao.stream/oldest` before\n   506\t  the first append — and keeps it, exactly as a composition that intends to\n   507\t  observe events caused by an operation mints `:dao.stream/newest` before\n   508\t  invoking it. A kept origin cursor converts a silent loss into a reported\n   509\t  `gap`. It cannot be minted afterwards and cannot be reconstructed: a consumer\n   510\t  that arrives later holds no evidence about what preceded it. On a transport\n   511\t  that declares complete retention this is unnecessary — a fresh `:oldest` is\n   512\t  the origin there, however late the consumer arrives.\n   513\t- Every valid cursor comes from the stream: minted by `cursor`, received as\n   514\t  a successor from `next`, or recovered from a gap outcome. Consumers never\n   515\t  construct cursor internals or fabricate positions — there is no `seek`. A\n   516\t  cursor's representation belongs to the transport.\n   517\t- A cursor binds to the logical stream, not to a handle: any handle on the\n   518\t  same logical stream accepts it, so a cursor handed to another interpreter\n   519\t  works with that interpreter's own handle. Whether a cursor survives\n   520\t  serialization to another host is transport-owned and TBD, like the\n   521\t  descriptor envelope.\n   522\t- A cursor carries the logical-stream identity, not a reachability descriptor.\n   523\t  `cursor-mismatch` compares that identity, so handles reached through distinct\n   524\t  descriptors for the same logical stream accept the same cursor.\n   525\t- Advancing is receiving a successor cursor from `next`. The stream never\n   526\t  advances a reader; the stream holds no reader positions at all. Dropping a\n   527\t  cursor is how a reader detaches — there is nothing to unregister.\n   528\t- Because cursors are immutable values, a reader may retain an old cursor and\n   529\t  re-read retained history, or hand a cursor to another interpreter. What a\n   530\t  cursor can still observe is governed only by retention.\n   531\t\n   532\t## Reading\n   533\t\n   534\t`next` takes a handle and a cursor and returns an outcome immediately.\n   535\tIt is total and non-blocking: every state of the stream has a defined data\n   677\tthat must know mints one at origin rather than asking later (see *Cursors*).\n   678\t\n   679\t### Complete history\n   680\t\n   681\tSome consumers require *complete* history rather than *retained* history: an\n   682\tinterpreter that derives state by replaying a log from its beginning is wrong,\n   683\tnot merely stale, if it replays a suffix. The contract already carries what\n   684\tsuch a consumer needs, in two mechanisms that answer different questions.\n   685\t\n   686\t**A transport that excludes `gap` gives completeness.** This is the existing\n   687\tdeclaration in its existing form — the outcome's precondition is impossible by\n   688\tthe transport's nature, an unbounded log never evicts, so never reports `gap`\n   689\t(see *Surfaces*). Retention is declared through the transport's nature and, for\n   690\ta locally created stream, through its creation specification; it is\n   691\tconfiguration provenance. There is no way to ask a handle about it and none is\n   692\tadded. A consumer requiring complete history therefore requires *a transport of\n   693\tthat declared nature*, and the composition that wires it is what supplies one.\n   694\t\n   695\t**A kept origin cursor gives detection.** On a transport that can evict, an\n   696\torigin cursor turns a loss that would otherwise be invisible into a reported\n   697\t`gap`, and the consumer aborts or recovers on its own policy. It does not make\n   698\tthe history complete; it makes incompleteness observable.\n   699\t\n   700\tBoth stand, and they are not substitutes. Where completeness is a correctness\n   701\trequirement, only the declared transport delivers it, and a kept origin cursor\n   702\tis the honest failure mode that remains when completeness was not wired. Where\n   703\ta consumer can proceed on a suffix but must know that it is on one, the origin\n   704\tcursor is right and sufficient.\n   705\t\n   706\t**Wiring a log onto an evicting transport is a host assembly defect**, of the\n   707\tsame kind as wiring a deposit destination that can refuse. A durable log is not\n   708\ta window: if a consumer treats a stream as the record of everything that\n   709\thappened, the transport under it must declare that it retains everything.\n   710\tCapacity is the wrong knob for this — a larger window makes loss less likely\n   711\tand never makes it reported — so sizing never substitutes for the declaration.\n   712\t\n   713\tThe obligation a complete-history transport takes on is one sentence: **a\n   714\tcomplete-history reader begins at its logical sequence's origin and never\n   715\tevicts an element of that sequence.** Every element present at creation, and\n   716\tevery value an append places on that same sequence with `ok`, remains part of\n   717\tit for as long as the logical stream exists. If the medium cannot serve\n   718\tretained history it reports `transport-error`; it never silently substitutes a\n   719\tsuffix. Everything else follows. Its `:oldest` anchor stays at the origin, so\n   720\t`gap` is impossible and is excluded for that reason.\n   721\t\n   722\tWhat it owes on the writing side depends on what it is. A complete-history\n   723\ttransport need not be writable at all — a finite immutable history is a valid\n   724\tone, and owes no writer outcome. A writable one that declares a finite capacity\n   725\treturns `full` on reaching it, without appending and without evicting: refusing\n   726\tand evicting are the two answers to the same condition, and a transport that has\n   727\tgiven up one owes the other. A writable one with no declared capacity — a\n   728\tlogically unbounded log, however it grows or spills beneath — may exclude\n   729\t`full`. Logically unbounded is not physically infinite: unexpected exhaustion of\n   730\tthe underlying medium is `transport-error`, never the eviction of acknowledged\n   731\thistory.\n   732\t\n   733\t## Composition\n   734\t\n   735\tDaoStream defines only streams, cursors, and the operations above. Anything\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
